import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"data is a nominal former + constructor arrows + dependent eliminator — design detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"`data` is a nominal former + constructor arrows + dependent eliminator — design detail","type":"design","date":"2026-10-21T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","refs":["ADR-0152"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/0152-data-nominal-inductive-families.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/0152-data-nominal-inductive-families.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/design/0152-data-nominal-inductive-families.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="data-is-a-nominal-former-constructor-arrows-dependent-eliminator-—-design-detail" tabindex="-1"><code>data</code> is a nominal former + constructor arrows + dependent eliminator — design detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-is-a-nominal-former-constructor-arrows-dependent-eliminator-—-design-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “data is a nominal former + constructor arrows + dependent eliminator — design detail”">​</a></h1><p>Moved verbatim from ADR-0152 (ADR-0161 size budget). The ADR carries the rules; this page carries the context, tables, phases and trade-offs.</p><h2 id="context-—-what-data-does-today-and-why-it-is-confusing" tabindex="-1">Context — what <code>data</code> does today and why it is confusing <a class="header-anchor" href="#context-—-what-data-does-today-and-why-it-is-confusing" aria-label="Permalink to “Context — what data does today and why it is confusing”">​</a></h2><p><code>desugar.data</code> (<code>engine-transform-rewrite/src/native/desugar.rs</code>) lowers <code>data Nat :: Zero | Succ(pred : Nat)</code> into a block of three kinds of bindings:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>-- 1. THE TYPE ITSELF IS A PREDICATE ARROW</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Nat := __v ~&gt; loop(...)                      -- folds over v, checks tags</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- 2. constructors are arrows producing string-tagged objects</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Zero := {&quot;tag&quot;: &quot;Zero&quot;}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Succ := pred ~&gt; {&quot;tag&quot;: &quot;Succ&quot;, &quot;pred&quot;: pred}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- 3. the eliminator is a first-order fold</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Nat.rec := (motive, cZero, cSucc) ~&gt; &lt;fold, decreasing on \`pred\`&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This encoding has three conflations that make it hard to reason about:</p><ol><li><strong>The type is a runtime predicate, not a type.</strong> <code>Nat</code> is an arrow that inspects <code>{&quot;tag&quot;: ...}</code> objects. &quot;Being a Nat&quot; (a static fact) is conflated with &quot;passing a tag-checking fold&quot; (a runtime test). <code>x : Nat</code> cannot mean &quot;<code>Nat</code>&#39;s parent chain contains <code>x</code>&quot; — it means &quot;calling <code>Nat(x)</code> returned true&quot;, an entirely different notion that only works for tag-shaped values and says nothing statically.</li><li><strong>Constructor identity is a string.</strong> <code>{&quot;tag&quot;: &quot;Succ&quot;}</code> compares structurally; the nominal <code>sym</code> machinery (ADR-0106) does not apply, so <code>match</code> dispatch is a string compare and two unrelated <code>data</code> declarations with a same-named ctor collide in tag space.</li><li><strong>The eliminator is not dependent.</strong> <code>Nat.rec</code> folds to a <em>value</em>; its motive is not <code>Nat ~&gt; Type</code>. Induction — &quot;to prove <code>P(n)</code> for all <code>n</code>, supply <code>P(Zero)</code> and <code>P(n) ~&gt; P(Succ n)</code>&quot; — is not expressible. The checker&#39;s dependent <code>match</code> index-refinement (<code>synth_match_arms</code>, <code>eq_n</code> path witnesses) has <em>no corresponding value-level eliminator</em>: it is a bespoke checker special-case, which is also why index normalization (<code>Vec(1+1)</code> vs <code>Vec(2)</code>) needs a custom rewriting solver instead of falling out of computation.</li><li><strong>Strict positivity lives in the desugar native</strong>, a syntactic pre-check the checker must trust — the checker itself never sees constructor signatures in a form it can verify.</li></ol><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><code>data F(i₁…iₖ : I₁…Iₖ) :: C₁ : S₁ | … | Cₙ : Sₙ</code> elaborates into three declarations in the ordinary object/arrow world. All three are values and types of the kind ADR-0106 already defines — no new machinery.</p><h3 id="_1-f-is-a-nominal-former" tabindex="-1">1. <code>F</code> is a nominal former <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-f-is-a-nominal-former" aria-label="Permalink to “1. F is a nominal former”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>F : Pi(i₁ : I₁, …, Pi(iₖ : Iₖ, Type))</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>F</code> binds a <code>sym</code> nominal (the former&#39;s identity). Applying it does <strong>not</strong> mint a fresh <code>sym</code> per call — it produces the canonical <em>family-application</em> object:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Vec(2, Int)  ==  { &quot;__former__&quot;: vecSym, &quot;indices&quot;: [2, Int] }</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Family equality is then ordinary: same former identity + index equality. <code>Vec ≠ List</code> because <code>vecSym ≠ listSym</code> (nominal); <code>Vec(2, Int) == Vec(2, Int)</code> because the applications are equal data. This also resolves the generic-instantiation memoization question left open in ADR-0106 §9 (<code>List&lt;Int&gt; == List&lt;Int&gt;</code>): a family application is canonical data, not a per-call symbol mint.</p><h3 id="_2-each-constructor-is-a-nominal-arrow-into-the-family" tabindex="-1">2. Each constructor is a nominal arrow into the family <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-each-constructor-is-a-nominal-arrow-into-the-family" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Each constructor is a nominal arrow into the family”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Cⱼ : Pi(x₁ : A₁, …, xₘ : Aₘ, F(j₁…jₖ))</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>Cⱼ</code> binds a <code>sym</code> (the constructor&#39;s nominal identity — <code>match</code> dispatches on <em>identity</em>, not on a <code>{&quot;tag&quot;}</code> string). Application produces a tagged object carrying the ctor sym and the field values:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Succ(Zero)  ==  { &quot;__ctor__&quot;: succSym, &quot;pred&quot;: Zero }</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Membership is <em>declared</em>: <code>type_of(Cⱼ(…))</code> is <code>F(j⃗)</code> by the ctor&#39;s result type — no tag inspection, no chain walk. <code>Succ(3)</code> fails to check because <code>3 : Nat</code> fails; <code>Succ(Zero)</code> checks and yields <code>Nat</code>.</p><h3 id="_3-f-rec-is-the-dependent-eliminator" tabindex="-1">3. <code>F.rec</code> is the dependent eliminator <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-f-rec-is-the-dependent-eliminator" aria-label="Permalink to “3. F.rec is the dependent eliminator”">​</a></h3><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>F.rec : Pi(m    : Pi(i⃗ : I⃗, F(i⃗) ~&gt; Type),          -- the motive</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>          Pi(c₁ : S₁[rec], …,                       -- one case per ctor</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>          Pi(cₙ : Sₙ[rec],</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>          Pi(i⃗ : I⃗, Pi(v : F(i⃗), m(i⃗, v))))))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>where <code>Sⱼ[rec]</code> is <code>Cⱼ</code>&#39;s signature with a <code>Pi(x_rec, m(…, field))</code> argument inserted after each recursive field. For <code>Nat</code>:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Nat.rec : Pi(m   : Nat ~&gt; Type,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>            Pi(cZ : m(Zero),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>            Pi(cS : Pi(p : Nat, m(p) ~&gt; m(Succ p)),</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>            Pi(n : Nat, m(n)))))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It <strong>computes</strong> — iota reduction is definitional:</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Nat.rec(m, cZ, cS, Zero)    ⟶  cZ</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Nat.rec(m, cZ, cS, Succ p)  ⟶  cS(p, Nat.rec(m, cZ, cS, p))</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>match</code> is surface sugar: <code>match v { Zero: z, Succ(p): s p }</code> lowers to <code>Nat.rec((n) ~&gt; &lt;result type&gt;, z, (p, ih) ~&gt; s p, v)</code> — and the dependent reading (motive <code>m</code> may use the scrutinee <code>n</code>) is what per-arm index refinement already implements in <code>synth_match_arms</code>: the refinement is no longer a checker special-case, it is the type of <code>F.rec</code>&#39;s cases. <code>absurd</code> remains: an arm is inhabited only if its refined index equations are satisfiable.</p><p>Iota is also where index equality comes from: <code>Vec(1+1)</code> ≡ <code>Vec(2)</code> because <code>1+1</code> <em>evaluates</em> to <code>2</code> under the shared evaluator — the bespoke index-rewriting solver becomes a normalization question, not a separate algorithm.</p><h3 id="positivity-and-termination-move-to-the-checker" tabindex="-1">Positivity and termination move to the checker <a class="header-anchor" href="#positivity-and-termination-move-to-the-checker" aria-label="Permalink to “Positivity and termination move to the checker”">​</a></h3><p>Strict positivity is checked when the <code>data</code> declaration&#39;s ctor signatures are elaborated — the checker has the signatures, so it owns the check; the <code>desugar.data</code> native&#39;s positivity pass is retired (or kept only as an early-error mirror). Termination of <code>F.rec</code> uses the existing decreasing-argument metadata: <code>F.rec</code> is structurally decreasing on <code>v</code>&#39;s ctor spine by construction.</p><h2 id="worked-example-—-data-nat-zero-succ-pred-nat" tabindex="-1">Worked example — <code>data Nat :: Zero | Succ(pred : Nat)</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#worked-example-—-data-nat-zero-succ-pred-nat" aria-label="Permalink to “Worked example — data Nat :: Zero | Succ(pred : Nat)”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Elaborates to</th><th>Checks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Nat</code></td><td><code>natSym : Type</code></td><td><code>Nat : Type</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Zero</code></td><td><code>{&quot;__ctor__&quot;: zeroSym} : Nat</code></td><td><code>Zero : Nat</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Succ</code></td><td><code>Pi(pred : Nat, Nat)</code></td><td><code>Succ(Zero) : Nat</code>, <code>Succ(3)</code> ✗</td></tr><tr><td><code>match n { Zero: 0, Succ(p): p }</code></td><td><code>Nat.rec((_)~&gt;Nat, 0, (p,_)~&gt;p, n)</code></td><td><code>: Nat</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>plus0 : Pi(n:Nat, Path(n+0, n))</code></td><td><code>Nat.rec((n)~&gt;Path(n+0,n), refl, (p,ih)~&gt;…, n)</code></td><td>a <em>proof</em> by induction — the same eliminator</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>match</code> on an indexed family (<code>Vec(n, a)</code> scrutinee) needs no new rule: checking <code>Vec.rec</code>&#39;s <code>cVNil : m(0, VNil)</code> case unifies the scrutinee&#39;s index with <code>0</code> for that arm — the <code>eq_n</code> witness is the transport the type of <code>m</code> demands, not a bespoke binding.</p><h2 id="membership-vs-subtyping-caveat-normative" tabindex="-1">Membership vs subtyping caveat (normative) <a class="header-anchor" href="#membership-vs-subtyping-caveat-normative" aria-label="Permalink to “Membership vs subtyping caveat (normative)”">​</a></h2><p>Under <code>sym</code>&#39;s single parent chain, <code>x : T</code> and <code>T &lt;: U</code> must be read differently or <code>Zero instanceof Type</code> comes out true (Zero→Nat→Int→…→Type):</p><ul><li><code>x : T</code> — the <strong>head</strong> of <code>x</code>&#39;s nominal parent chain is <code>T</code> (direct membership; for ctor values this is the declared family).</li><li><code>T &lt;: U</code> — <code>U</code> occurs in <code>T</code>&#39;s parent chain <strong>restricted to type nodes</strong> (subtyping).</li><li><code>x : T</code> then propagates through supertypes: <code>x : T</code> and <code>T &lt;: U</code> gives <code>x : U</code> — the judgment the checker implements, not a single <code>instanceof</code> walk.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Removed</strong>: type-as-predicate arrows, <code>{&quot;tag&quot;}</code> string dispatch, the <code>Nat := __v ~&gt; loop</code> membership encoding, the bespoke match-index-refinement special-casing, the index-normalization solver&#39;s raison d&#39;être (it becomes ordinary normalization).</li><li><strong>Kept</strong>: surface syntax (<code>data … :: … | …</code>), <code>match</code>/<code>absurd</code> surface, <code>Type.rec</code> naming, strict-positivity enforcement (moved to the checker), decreasing-argument termination metadata.</li><li><strong>New capability</strong>: <code>F.rec</code> being dependent means <em>proofs by induction are ordinary arrows</em> — <code>Pi(n:Nat, Path(n+0,n))</code> is inhabited by <code>Nat.rec</code> with motive <code>(n) ~&gt; Path(n+0, n)</code>. Proofs require nothing beyond <code>data</code> + <code>Pi</code> + <code>Path</code>.</li><li><strong>Migration</strong>: <code>lib-type</code> and consumers minting <code>{&quot;tag&quot;: …}</code> values for <code>data</code> types move to ctor-sym objects; the <code>__tag__</code> bridge (ADR-0106 conformance note) covers the transition period.</li><li><strong>Boundary</strong>: <code>data</code> declarations inside signatures erase at exec per ADR-0151 — the former/ctor/rec bindings are what exec sees; the family/ctor <em>types</em> are typecheck-owned metadata.</li></ul>`,37)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};